WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.423
This video is about how software can search by meaning instead of exact words.

00:00:05.623 --> 00:00:09.501
A customer writes: my computer can't see the gadget once it wakes up.

00:00:09.751 --> 00:00:13.408
Your keyword search looks for those exact words and finds nothing.

00:00:13.658 --> 00:00:30.574
BM25 (keyword search): The classic keyword-search ranking formula: scores a document higher when it contains the query words, especially rare ones, without rewarding endless repetition. Like: grep with judgement: rare words like an error code count far more than "the".

00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:39.570
Yet the help article titled dock not detected after sleep is exactly the answer. Embeddings are how software notices that these mean the same thing.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:55.447
Embedding: A list of numbers (a vector) that represents the meaning of a piece of text, made so that texts with similar meaning get nearby vectors. Like: GPS coordinates for meaning: "puppy" and "dog" are neighbours, "invoice" is in another city.

00:00:56.547 --> 00:01:03.060
Imagine every sentence has a location on a giant map, where sentences that mean similar things sit near each other.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:11.959
An embedding model turns a piece of text into a list of numbers, often hundreds or a few thousand of them. That list is the text's coordinates on the map.

00:01:12.359 --> 00:01:22.680
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:31.707
Dock not detected after sleep, and my computer can't see the gadget once it wakes, land close together, even though they share almost no words.

00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:37.726
So search becomes geometry: turn the question into coordinates, and find the nearest stored texts.

00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:40.575
Here is the whole pipeline.

00:01:40.975 --> 00:01:44.841
Ahead of time, you embed every chunk of every document once.

00:01:45.241 --> 00:02:00.845
Chunking: Cutting documents into smaller passages (chunks) before indexing, so a search returns just the relevant part rather than a whole manual. Like: Splitting a firmware image into flash pages: small units you can find and fetch individually.

00:02:01.145 --> 00:02:05.336
You store those vectors in an index, often inside a vector database.

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:23.488
Vector: An ordered list of numbers, such as [0.12, -0.80, 0.33]. In this course, almost always an embedding. Like: A fixed-size array of floats, exactly like float v[1536] in C.

00:02:23.788 --> 00:02:43.919
Vector database: A database that stores embeddings and quickly finds the ones closest to a query vector. Examples: pgvector (a PostgreSQL extension), Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma. Like: A map app that answers "what are the 5 nearest coffee shops to this point?" in milliseconds.

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:47.633
When a question arrives, you embed it with the same model.

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:58.702
And you return the stored vectors closest to it. Closeness is usually measured with cosine similarity, which checks whether two arrows point in the same direction, ignoring their length.

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:19.408
Cosine similarity: A score from -1 to 1 for how closely two vectors point in the same direction: dot product divided by the product of their lengths. Near 1 means similar meaning. Like: Comparing two compass headings: same direction scores 1, opposite scores -1, regardless of distance travelled.

00:03:20.508 --> 00:03:23.620
Embeddings are not magic. Here is where they struggle.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:33.191
Rare exact strings, like the error code E-4012 or a part number, get blurred, because the model learned meaning, not serial numbers.

00:03:33.591 --> 00:03:38.201
Classic keyword search, such as BM25, matches those exactly.

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:45.009
So production systems run both searches and merge the two result lists. That combination is called hybrid search.

00:03:45.409 --> 00:04:02.755
Hybrid search: Running keyword search and semantic search together and merging the two ranked lists, so you catch both exact identifiers and paraphrases. Like: Asking both the parts catalogue (exact part numbers) and the experienced tech (meaning), then combining their answers.

00:04:03.055 --> 00:04:13.283
And you measure it: for a set of real questions, did the right document make the top results? In lab six, hybrid search beats either method alone on exactly this kind of data.

00:04:14.483 --> 00:04:21.275
Question for you: Why does a search for E-4012 still need keyword matching, even with good embeddings?

00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:39.498
Answer: Embeddings capture meaning, but they blur rare exact strings such as error codes and part numbers. Keyword search matches them exactly, so production systems combine both.

00:04:40.598 --> 00:04:41.248
Recap.

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:44.864
An embedding is a text's coordinates on a map of meaning.

00:04:45.264 --> 00:04:50.117
Semantic search finds the nearest vectors, usually by cosine similarity.

00:04:50.517 --> 00:05:04.461
Semantic search: Search by meaning using embeddings: "device won't charge" can find a passage that says "battery fails to take power". Like: A librarian who understands what you mean, not just which words you typed.

00:05:04.761 --> 00:05:08.407
Exact codes and part numbers need keyword search as well.

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:13.764
And you measure retrieval quality instead of guessing. Lab two builds this from scratch.
